  "confidence": 0.94,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["SW2", "R81", "R53"],
      "pins": ["SW2.1", "SW2.2", "SW2.3", "SW2.4", "U2.6", "U2.7", "U19.6", "U19.7"],
      "nets": ["/NET0020", "/NET0192", "/NET0167", "/NET0067", "Net-(R81-Pad2)", "Net-(R53-Pad2)"],
      "observed": "SW2 is a two-pole DIP switch. One pole connects the far ends of R81 and R53, which are each 120 ohms from different CANL nets; the other pole directly connects the CANH nets of U2 and U19. Closing the corresponding switch poles therefore couples the two CAN interfaces.",
      "expected": "The specification describes two isolated, independent CAN buses. Their CANH/CANL signal pairs should remain separate; any termination switching should terminate each bus locally rather than join the buses.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (name \"/NET0020\") ... (node (ref \"U2\") (pin \"6\") (pinfunction \"CANL\")) ... (node (ref \"R81\") (pin \"1\")))"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (name \"/NET0192\") ... (node (ref \"U19\") (pin \"6\") (pinfunction \"CANL\")) ... (node (ref \"R53\") (pin \"1\")))"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (name \"/NET0167\") ... (node (ref \"U2\") (pin \"7\") (pinfunction \"CANH\")) ... (node (ref \"SW2\") (pin \"4\")))"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (name \"/NET0067\") ... (node (ref \"U19\") (pin \"7\") (pinfunction \"CANH\")) ... (node (ref \"SW2\") (pin \"3\")))"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (name \"Net-(R81-Pad2)\") ... (node (ref \"SW2\") (pin \"1\")))"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (name \"Net-(R53-Pad2)\") ... (node (ref \"SW2\") (pin \"2\")))"
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "two isolated CAN buses (CAN0, CAN1), each driven by its own MCP2515 SPI CAN controller"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "With the switch poles closed, traffic or faults on one CAN interface can affect the other, defeating the intended bus separation and potentially disrupting communication.",
      "confidence": 0.96
    },
    {
      "category": "polarity",
      "components": ["Q7", "U1"],
      "pins": ["Q7.2", "Q7.3", "Q7.1", "U1.1", "U1.5", "U1.6", "J11.A4"],
      "nets": ["+5V", "NET0183", "Net-(Q7-G)"],
      "observed": "Q7's source (pin 2) is connected to +5V, while its drain (pin 3) is connected to USB VBUS on NET0183. U1's VIN is on NET0183 and SENSE is on +5V.",
      "expected": "For the LTC4412 ideal-diode path from USB VBUS (VIN) to the +5V output (SENSE), the P-channel MOSFET should have its source on the input side and its drain on the output side, not the reverse.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (name \"+5V\") ... (node (ref \"Q7\") (pin \"2\") (pinfunction \"S\")) ... (node (ref \"U1\") (pin \"6\") (pinfunction \"SENSE\")))"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (name \"NET0183\") ... (node (ref \"J11\") (pin \"A4\") (pinfunction \"VBUS\")) ... (node (ref \"Q7\") (pin \"3\") (pinfunction \"D\")) ... (node (ref \"U1\") (pin \"1\") (pinfunction \"VIN\")))"
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "\"USB diode doesn't work, I guess need ideal diode to handle more power? LTC4412 controller and p-ch mosfet. need reverse current protection mostly\""
        }
      ],
      "impact": "The reversed MOSFET body-diode direction can allow USB VBUS to feed the +5V rail without controlled ideal-diode operation, compromising the stated reverse-current protection.",
      "confidence": 0.91
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The netlist shows SW2 can couple the two CAN interfaces, contrary to the specified independent isolated buses. It also places the ideal-diode P-MOSFET with source and drain reversed relative to the LTC4412 input/output path, potentially undermining reverse-current protection."
}

